<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kłodzko</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Glatz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kłodzko, in German Glatz, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a> Kłodzka at the centre of a basin enclosed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetes">Sudetes</a>. For centuries it belonged to the Bohemian crown, guarding the road from Silesia into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it in 1742. Its hilltop fortress was rebuilt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>, and the town passed to Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klodzko.jpg" alt="Kłodzko" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacek Halicki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2024_Szko%C5%82a_Podstawowa_Nr_1_w_K%C5%82odzku_(13).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kłodzko stands in the centre of the Kłodzko Valley, on the Eastern Neisse, and is the seat of Kłodzko County. The area has been populated since at least the 1st century BCE, and archaeological sites suggest a settlement on the ancient Amber Road that traded widely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. It was established as a settlement in the 10th century and received city rights in 1233. Culturally and traditionally the town belongs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest mention is in Cosmas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>'s Chronicle of Bohemians, which names Cladzco as belonging in 981 to Duke Slavník, father of Adalbert of Prague. The town passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in a series of conflicts, and in 1114 the Bohemian Duke Soběslav I captured and burnt it, then rebuilt it. By the Peace of Kłodzko of 1137 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> of Poland gave up all claims to the Kłodzko Land. A Mongol raid reached the town in 1241, and King Wenceslaus I drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> out. In 1334 Bohemia bought the town back from the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Ziębice</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of Luxembourg relocated it, which brought fast growth and German settlers.</p><p>In 1459 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a> raised the whole Kłodzko Land to a county, and in 1526 it passed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> the fortress was besieged several times between 1619 and 1649. It was never captured, but over 900 of the town's 1,300 buildings were destroyed and the population fell by more than half. The Austrian authorities then ended local self-government and made it a small garrison town attached to the growing fortress. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> annexed Glatz during the Silesian Wars and continued the fortress, and the town joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the fortress was a prison and a subcamp of the Gross-Rosen concentration camp. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took the town without a major battle on 9 May 1945, and under the Potsdam Conference's border changes it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Its German inhabitants were deported in February 1946, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> settled the town. It is the commercial centre of its valley and counted 25,717 inhabitants in 2021. Its Gothic stone bridge of 1390, often called a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-bridge">Charles Bridge</a> in miniature, survived the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kłodzko” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Kłodzko</b> (Glatz) Land, a basin enclosed by the Sudeten mountains, had long belonged to Bohemia, though at times it was held by Silesian dukes.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-kladsko">County of Kladsko</a></li></ul></section>
